This project shows how to set up and check the health of a Kubernetes based system that runs multiple small applications (microservices). It includes the configuration files needed to deploy everything, along with a reusable Python script that checks whether the system is working properly.

In simple terms, the goal of this project is to show that I can:

Look inside a Kubernetes cluster and understand what is running

Check Pods, Deployments, and Services

Identify applications that are not starting, not responding, or not working correctly

Also,Test whether services are reachable through HTTP (This was Optional)

Work confidently with Kubernetes configuration files and troubleshoot issues



Project Structure

The project folder contains:

k8s_health_check.py – A Python script that checks the health of the cluster

whereami.yaml – Configuration for deploying the Whereami application

prom.yaml – Configuration for deploying Prometheus

ingress.yaml – Configuration for routing traffic using NGINX Ingress

README.md – Project documentation



What Was Deployed in Kubernetes
1. For Whereami Application

Deployment with 6 replicas (6 running copies of the app)

ClusterIP Service on port 80

Ingress configured to route traffic to /whereami

In some cases, the Whereami Pods may stay in a Pending state. This usually happens if the local Kubernetes cluster (such as Minikube) does not have enough CPU or memory allocated. Increasing the available resources fixes this issue.


2. Redis (Database Component)

Redis Master Deployment

Redis Replica Deployment

Separate ClusterIP Services for both

In environments with limited resources, the Redis replica Pods may fail readiness checks. This means they start but are not fully ready to serve traffic.


3. Prometheus (Monitoring Tool)

Deployment with 1 replica

ClusterIP Service on port 8080

Custom ConfigMap for scrape configuration

Ingress route available at /prom

Prometheus was used to monitor and collect metrics from the applications running in the cluster.


4. Ingress Controller

The NGINX Ingress Controller was enabled using the Minikube addon.

It handles incoming HTTP requests and routes them to the correct application based on the URL path.



Kubernetes Health Check Script:

The k8s_health_check.py script is a reusable Python tool that connects to the Kubernetes cluster and checks:

Pod status (Running, Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, etc.)

Deployment replica availability

Service configuration and exposed ports

(Optional) HTTP endpoint checks (for example, testing Prometheus or application URLs)

The script used the kubeconfig file to authenticate and can be reused with different Kubernetes clusters.



Requirements:

Python

Kubernetes Python client

Requests library



It is recommended to use a virtual environment:

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install kubernetes requests
How to Run the Project.



First, apply the Kubernetes configuration files:

kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml
kubectl apply -f prom.yaml
kubectl apply -f whereami.yaml


Then run the health check script:

python3 k8s_health_check.py



The script will display:

Pod states (Running, Pending, CrashLoopBackOff)

Deployment replica status

Service port details

Whether HTTP endpoints are reachable



What Was Observed in Testing (Minikube Environment)

From testing in a local Minikube cluster:

The frontend and redis master Pods were healthy.

The redis-replica Pods showed containers not fully ready.

The whereami Pods were in a Pending state, most likely due to insufficient memory allocation.

The Prometheus deployment was running successfully.


To test HTTP endpoints locally, port forwarding is required:

kubectl port-forward deployment/prometheus-deployment 9090:9090 -n monitoring
kubectl port-forward svc/whereami 8080:80

This allows access to the services through localhost for validation.